  "fleiss_vs_alpha": {
    "note": "Fleiss' kappa is only defined when every unit has the same number of raters. CREMA-D cells hold 6-12, so kappa is computed on the exactly-10 subset and alpha is recomputed on that same subset for a like-for-like comparison. They differ for two reasons: alpha's expected disagreement carries an (n-1) small-sample correction that kappa's does not, and alpha is defined over the whole ragged design while kappa has to throw 32% of the cells away.",
